WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.666
Welcome to Gander Oceanic.

00:00:05.666 --> 00:00:09.051
Your application has been reviewed and accepted by senior staff,

00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:13.125
so you are now a student, and this course is the next stage of your training.

00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:15.778
It's for controllers who already hold a C1 rating,

00:00:15.778 --> 00:00:18.325
so you don't need to learn control from scratch.

00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:22.369
Your job now is to learn how oceanic control works, and ours is to teach you.

00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:25.365
Here's where you are on the path.

00:00:25.365 --> 00:00:29.477
Your application and the senior staff review are done, and you've been accepted.

00:00:29.477 --> 00:00:33.126
The Training Hub on the Gander website shows your progress at any time.

00:00:33.126 --> 00:00:35.182
Next, you prepare for the entrance exam.

00:00:35.182 --> 00:00:38.368
Everything in it is in the Knowledge Base and in these videos.

00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:41.695
Then you take the exam when you're ready.

00:00:41.695 --> 00:00:45.402
It's twenty multiple-choice questions, and the pass mark is eighty percent.

00:00:45.402 --> 00:00:47.801
If you don't pass, you can retake after one day,

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.950
and after seven days for any later attempt.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.004
Once you've passed, you submit your availability so an instructor can pick you up.

00:00:55.304 --> 00:00:59.252
Finally, you have a live session with that instructor on the network.

00:00:59.252 --> 00:01:03.229
Under a shared agreement with VATSIM UK, Oceanic students are trained

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:06.976
for both the Gander and the Shanwick sides of the North Atlantic.

00:01:06.976 --> 00:01:11.096
So your live session is taken on NAT_FSS, the position that covers both.

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:14.100
That way, every competence you need is explained and

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.989
understood before an Oceanic endorsement is given.

00:01:16.989 --> 00:01:21.623
If the instructor passes you, you receive your endorsement and your Discord role.

00:01:22.923 --> 00:01:24.156
There are three videos.

00:01:24.156 --> 00:01:28.337
This one covers both airspaces, Gander and Shanwick, and who you'll work with.

00:01:28.337 --> 00:01:29.892
The second covers procedures.

00:01:29.892 --> 00:01:32.143
The third covers communications and tools.

00:01:32.143 --> 00:01:33.483
Let's start with the map.

00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:38.593
The Gander Oceanic FIR borders Shanwick along thirty degrees west.

00:01:38.593 --> 00:01:43.355
It borders Santa Maria at forty-five north, between thirty and forty degrees west,

00:01:43.355 --> 00:01:48.176
and New York Oceanic at forty-five north, between forty and fifty-one degrees west.

00:01:48.176 --> 00:01:52.447
On the land side, it borders Gander Domestic, Montreal, Edmonton and Nuuk.

00:01:53.747 --> 00:01:58.893
All the airspace in the FIR from flight level fifty-five up to unlimited is Class A.

00:01:58.893 --> 00:02:01.834
That's the Gander OCA, the Oceanic Control Area.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:05.203
Below flight level fifty-five, the airspace is Class G.

00:02:06.503 --> 00:02:10.442
One more area matters: the Gander Oceanic Transition Area, or GOTA.

00:02:10.442 --> 00:02:14.353
This is airspace that Gander Oceanic delegates to Gander Domestic,

00:02:14.353 --> 00:02:17.909
so the domestic radar controller handles it rather than you.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:19.966
Also remember the transition level.

00:02:19.966 --> 00:02:24.317
In the North Atlantic oceanic control areas it is flight level fifty-five.

00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:26.798
Now the Shanwick side.

00:02:26.798 --> 00:02:29.776
The Shanwick FIR borders Gander at thirty degrees west,

00:02:29.776 --> 00:02:33.349
Santa Maria at forty-five north, and Reykjavik at sixty-one north.

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:36.875
On the eastern side it borders the domestic airspace of Scottish,

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:38.719
Shannon, London, Brest and Madrid.

00:02:38.719 --> 00:02:42.731
As in Gander, the airspace from flight level fifty-five upward is Class A,

00:02:42.731 --> 00:02:44.250
and that's the Shanwick OCA.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.075
Below it, the airspace is Class G.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:50.043
Shanwick also delegates three transition areas,

00:02:50.043 --> 00:02:53.222
all above flight level fifty-five, to radar controllers.

00:02:53.222 --> 00:02:55.322
The Northern Oceanic Transition Area,

00:02:55.322 --> 00:02:59.124
NOTA, and the Shannon Oceanic Transition Area, SOTA, go to Shannon.

00:02:59.124 --> 00:03:02.216
The Brest Oceanic Transition Area, BOTA, goes to Brest.

00:03:02.216 --> 00:03:04.375
Just like the GOTA on the Gander side,

00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:07.557
these areas belong to the radar controllers, not to you.

00:03:08.857 --> 00:03:13.250
Gander Oceanic Control manages the en-route traffic in the Gander OCA.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:18.333
The main position is CZQO_FSS, and on the radio, Gander is known as Gander Radio.

00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:20.488
Shanwick follows the same pattern,

00:03:20.488 --> 00:03:24.735
with EGGX_CTR as its main position and the callsign Shanwick Radio.

00:03:24.735 --> 00:03:29.232
Each position has its own VHF alias frequency and its own HF frequency,

00:03:29.232 --> 00:03:31.513
and you'll find them all in the SOP.

00:03:32.813 --> 00:03:36.216
On major events, extra positions open on both sides: Alpha,

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:41.580
Bravo, Charlie, Delta and Foxtrot, plus the Delivery positions that issue oceanic clearances.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.820
We'll only skim over these, because you'll rarely see them outside events.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.138
The SOP lists them all.

00:03:47.138 --> 00:03:50.519
Every Gander position uses the same callsign: Gander Radio.

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:53.190
Two things to remember.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:57.218
CZQO_FSS must be staffed before any other Gander position can open,

00:03:57.218 --> 00:04:00.043
and on the Shanwick side, EGGX_CTR comes first.

00:04:00.043 --> 00:04:02.725
Then there's NAT_FSS, called the NAT Bandbox.

00:04:02.725 --> 00:04:06.990
When local controllers are offline, it covers both Shanwick and Gander,

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.114
using whichever callsign fits where the aircraft is.

00:04:10.114 --> 00:04:13.451
It's also the position you'll use for your live session.

00:04:14.751 --> 00:04:16.629
Because NAT routes change every day,

00:04:16.629 --> 00:04:19.812
there are no published sector boundaries between controllers.

00:04:19.812 --> 00:04:24.442
If you split the airspace north and south, you agree the split with the other controller,

00:04:24.442 --> 00:04:26.783
then coordinate it with the adjacent sectors.

00:04:26.783 --> 00:04:30.346
It's also common to add a clearance controller or a track controller.

00:04:31.646 --> 00:04:34.509
Now the handoff points, starting on the Gander side.

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:36.656
You'll hand traffic to five neighbours.

00:04:36.656 --> 00:04:39.628
To the north is Reykjavik, callsign Reykjavik Control.

00:04:39.628 --> 00:04:43.803
The minimum is ten nautical miles at and above flight level two-seven-zero,

00:04:43.803 --> 00:04:44.638
and five below.

00:04:45.938 --> 00:04:49.566
To the south-west is New York Oceanic, callsign New York Radio.

00:04:49.566 --> 00:04:51.870
It runs a procedural service with ADS-C.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:58.363
You hand off to its Mercury sector, and New York delegates a block of airspace north of forty-five thirty north,

00:04:58.363 --> 00:05:00.509
between forty and fifty west, to you.

00:05:01.809 --> 00:05:05.214
To the south is Santa Maria, callsign Santa Maria Radio.

00:05:05.214 --> 00:05:10.566
The lateral minimum is fifty nautical miles and the longitudinal minimum is ten minutes.

00:05:10.566 --> 00:05:12.817
You hand off to its non-radar sector.

00:05:14.117 --> 00:05:16.716
On the land side is Montreal, a radar service.

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:19.458
Its sectors split at flight level two-nine-zero,

00:05:19.458 --> 00:05:21.915
and the lower ones sit underneath the GOTA.

00:05:21.915 --> 00:05:23.723
The minimum there is five miles.

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:27.227
Last is Gander Domestic, also radar, with a five-mile minimum.

00:05:27.227 --> 00:05:32.143
When Gander Domestic is offline, Moncton Center covers its airspace from top to bottom.

00:05:33.443 --> 00:05:34.644
Now the Shanwick side.

00:05:34.644 --> 00:05:39.426
Reykjavik and Santa Maria also border Shanwick, with the same minima you've just heard,

00:05:39.426 --> 00:05:42.724
and you hand off to Santa Maria's non-radar sector here too.

00:05:42.724 --> 00:05:45.090
Reykjavik also controls the RATSU triangle,

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.657
which Scottish delegates to it at the junction of Reykjavik, Scottish and Shanwick.

00:05:50.957 --> 00:05:54.176
On the eastern edge, first is Scottish Control, a radar service.

00:05:54.176 --> 00:05:57.798
It borders Shanwick at ten degrees west, between Reykjavik and the NOTA.

00:05:57.798 --> 00:06:01.496
The minimum is seven nautical miles laterally for aircraft on ATS routes,

00:06:01.496 --> 00:06:05.042
and fifteen miles longitudinally, dropping to five with speed control.

00:06:05.042 --> 00:06:09.168
Shanwick coordinates east and westbound traffic at GOMUP, on the edge of the NOTA.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:13.293
If westbound traffic is going to Shannon rather than Shanwick, tell Scottish West.

00:06:14.593 --> 00:06:16.676
Next is Shannon Control, also radar.

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:20.940
It borders Shanwick from GOMUP counter-clockwise around its FIR to TULTA,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.159
and it controls the NOTA and the SOTA.

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:24.664
The minimum is five miles.

00:06:24.664 --> 00:06:26.921
There's one special transfer condition.

00:06:26.921 --> 00:06:29.661
For eastbound aircraft between SUNOT and BEDRA,

00:06:29.661 --> 00:06:34.966
control transfers at the sector boundary, and communications transfer one degree before it.

00:06:36.266 --> 00:06:39.985
Last are Brest and Madrid, both radar with a five-mile minimum.

00:06:39.985 --> 00:06:41.654
Brest Control runs the BOTA,

00:06:41.654 --> 00:06:45.888
and its main sector covers everything below flight level two-five-five.

00:06:45.888 --> 00:06:50.056
Madrid, callsign Madrid Radar, takes every handoff on its West sector,

00:06:50.056 --> 00:06:52.735
which is split at flight level two-four-five.

00:06:54.035 --> 00:06:57.077
Most traffic is deemed coordinated, which means you don't call.

00:06:57.077 --> 00:07:05.564
An aircraft entering your OCA is deemed coordinated if it's at its cleared level, will cross the entry point within three minutes of the last estimate or the time restriction,

00:07:05.564 --> 00:07:07.746
and the receiving controller hasn't objected.

00:07:07.746 --> 00:07:10.953
Leaving the OCA, the aircraft must be at its flight-planned level,

00:07:10.953 --> 00:07:15.326
with no level change in the last five minutes before the exit point and no known conflict.

00:07:16.626 --> 00:07:18.416
There are two ways to transfer a tag.

00:07:18.416 --> 00:07:22.019
With the silent method, you transfer communications together with the tag,

00:07:22.019 --> 00:07:26.060
and the receiving controller accepts the tag only once the aircraft has checked in.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:29.834
This is used between oceanic sectors, and between Shanwick and its neighbours.

00:07:29.834 --> 00:07:33.245
With the confirmed method, you start the tag transfer and wait for the

00:07:33.245 --> 00:07:36.462
receiving controller to accept before transferring communications.

00:07:36.462 --> 00:07:39.026
This is used between Gander and the domestic sectors.

00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:44.075
If you give a direct route that crosses a boundary, coordinate it,

00:07:44.075 --> 00:07:49.813
for example with the COPX function in EuroScope, and retype the direct into the datablock afterwards.

00:07:49.813 --> 00:07:50.942
That's the airspace.

00:07:50.942 --> 00:07:54.726
In the next video, we'll look at routes, clearances and separation.
